# CPU vs GPU

## Overview

Both the CPU and GPU are processors, but they are designed for different types of work.

- **CPU (Central Processing Unit)**: Built for general-purpose computing and complex decision-making.
- **GPU (Graphics Processing Unit)**: Built for performing many similar calculations simultaneously.

A modern computer typically uses **both**: the CPU manages the overall system, while the GPU accelerates graphics and parallel workloads.

## Key Differences

| Feature | CPU | GPU |
|----------|-----|-----|
| Full Form | Central Processing Unit | Graphics Processing Unit |
| Main Purpose | General-purpose processing | Parallel processing |
| Cores | Few powerful cores | Many smaller cores |
| Best At | Sequential and complex tasks | Repetitive tasks on large datasets |
| Optimized For | Low latency | High throughput |
| Common Uses | OS, applications, browsers, databases | Gaming, AI, video rendering, simulations |

## How They Work Together

The CPU and GPU are not competitors—they complement each other.

- The **CPU** runs the operating system and application logic.
- The **CPU** sends graphics or compute-intensive work to the GPU.
- The **GPU** processes that work much faster and returns the results.

**Example:**  
While playing a game, the CPU handles game logic and player input, while the GPU renders the graphics displayed on the screen.

## When to Use Which?

| Workload | Better Choice |
|----------|--------------|
| Running the operating system | CPU |
| Web browsing and office work | CPU |
| Compiling code | CPU |
| Gaming graphics | GPU |
| AI and machine learning | GPU |
| Video rendering | GPU |

## Quick Summary

- **CPU = Brain of the computer.**
- **GPU = Parallel processing engine.**
- CPU handles complex, sequential tasks.
- GPU handles many similar tasks at the same time.
- Modern applications often use both together for the best performance.